      "text": "To solve the problem of finding how many odd numbers between \\(100\\) and \\(999\\) have distinct digits, we will break it down step by step.\n\n1. **Identify the range of numbers:**\n   We are looking at three-digit numbers, so our range is from \\(100\\) to \\(999\\).\n\n2. **Determine the condition for an odd number:**\n   A number is odd if its last digit is odd. Therefore, the last digit must be one of the digits \\(1, 3, 5, 7,\\) or \\(9\\). Let’s denote the last digit as \\(d_3\\).\n\n3. **Choose the first digit \\(d_1\\):**\n   - The first digit \\(d_1\\) can be any digit from \\(1\\) to \\(9\\) except for \\(0\\) (if \\(d_3\\) is \\(0\\), \\(d_1\\) must also be chosen such that the resulting number remains within the range of three-digit numbers).\n   - Therefore, there are \\(9\\) choices for \\(d_1\\).\n\n4. **Choose the second digit \\(d_2\\):**\n   - The second digit \\(d_2\\) can be any digit from \\(0\\) to \\(9\\) except for the digit used as the first digit and the digit used as the last digit.\n   - Therefore, there are \\(8\\) choices for \\(d_2\\) (since we exclude \\(d_1\\) and \\(d_3\\)).\n\n5. **Calculate the total number of combinations:**\n   The total number of odd three-digit numbers with distinct digits is the product of the number of choices for each digit.\n   \\[\n   9 \\text{ (choices for } d_1\\text{)} \\times 8 \\text{ (choices for } d_2\\text{)} \\times 5 \\text{ (choices for } d_3\\text{)} = 360\n   \\]\n\n6. **Write the final answer:**\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{360}\n   \\]",
